\title{Exact Lower Bounds for the AVD-Total Chromatic Number of\\
Central Graphs of Regular Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the adjacent-vertex-distinguishing total chromatic number,
$\chi''_a$, of the central graph $C(G)$ of a regular graph $G$. A total
colouring assigns colours to both the vertices and the edges of a graph so that
incident and adjacent objects receive distinct colours; it is
\emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing} (AVD) when, in addition, any two adjacent
vertices have distinct \emph{colour sets} (the colour of the vertex together with
the colours of its incident edges). The central graph $C(G)$ is obtained from $G$
by subdividing every edge once and joining every pair of non-adjacent vertices.

For every $d$-regular graph $G$ with $d \ge 2$ that is not complete, we prove the
exact lower half of the guiding conjecture $\chi''_a(C(G)) = d + 3$: namely,
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge d + 3$. The argument rests on a counting bound
$|V(G)| \ge d + 2$; the fact that every original vertex of $C(G)$ has degree
$|V(G)| - 1$; an adjacent equal-degree obstruction forbidding
$(\Delta+1)$-colour AVD-total colourings when two adjacent vertices share the
maximum degree; and an upward-closure lemma. We also record the sharper bound
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$, which reveals that the conjectured equality can
hold only in the extremal regime $|V(G)| = d + 2$ (the cocktail-party graphs
$K_{d+2}$ minus a perfect matching) and fails already on the five-cycle. We close
with open problems, chief among them the matching upper bound in the extremal
case.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Colouring the elements of a graph subject to conflict constraints is a central
theme of combinatorics with applications to scheduling, frequency assignment,
and resource allocation. Two enrichments of classical colouring concern us here.

The first is \emph{total colouring}, in which vertices and edges are coloured
simultaneously and every pair of mutually incident or adjacent elements receives
distinct colours; the minimum number of colours is the total chromatic number
$\chi''(G)$. The trivial lower bound $\chi''(G) \ge \Delta(G) + 1$ holds because
a vertex of degree $\Delta$ and its incident edges form a clique of size
$\Delta + 1$ in the associated total graph.

The second is the \emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing} condition. To each vertex
$w$ we associate its colour set $C(w)$: the colour of $w$ together with the
colours of all edges incident to $w$. A total colouring is AVD if every pair of
adjacent vertices has distinct colour sets. The minimum number of colours over
all AVD-total colourings is the AVD-total chromatic number $\chi''_a(G)$.

The graphs we colour are \emph{central graphs}. The central graph $C(G)$ of a
simple graph $G$ is formed by subdividing each edge of $G$ once (introducing one
new subdivision vertex per edge) and adding an edge between every pair of
vertices non-adjacent in $G$. Every original vertex is then joined to all others,
attaining the maximum degree $|V(G)| - 1$, which makes central graphs a demanding
testbed for distinguishing colourings.

We restrict to \emph{regular} inputs. The guiding conjecture asserts a uniform
value.

\begin{conjecture}
For every $d$-regular graph $G$ with $d \ge 2$ that is not complete,
$\chi''_a(C(G)) = d + 3$.
\end{conjecture}

We establish the lower half of this conjecture exactly, for all such $G$, and
determine the precise regime in which the conjectured equality can hold.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $G = (V, E)$ is a finite simple graph, $\Delta(G)$ its maximum
degree, and $\Deg_G(v)$ the degree of $v$.

\begin{definition}[Total graph]
The \emph{total graph} $T(H)$ of a graph $H$ has vertex set
$V(H) \sqcup E(H)$. Two elements are adjacent in $T(H)$ when they are adjacent or
incident in $H$: two vertices $a,b$ iff $ab \in E(H)$; a vertex $a$ and an edge
$e$ iff $a \in e$; two edges $e,f$ iff $e \ne f$ and they share an endpoint.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total colouring]
A \emph{total colouring} of $H$ with palette $\kappa$ is a proper vertex colouring
of $T(H)$: adjacent elements of $T(H)$ receive distinct colours in $\kappa$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Colour set]
Given a total colouring $C$ and a vertex $w$, the \emph{colour set} of $w$ is
\[
C(w) = \{\text{colour of } w\} \cup \{\text{colour of } e : e \ni w\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[AVD-total colouring and chromatic number]
A total colouring $C$ is \emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing} (AVD) if
$C(a) \ne C(b)$ for every edge $ab \in E(H)$. The \emph{AVD-total chromatic
number} is
\[
\chi''_a(H) = \min\{n : H \text{ has an AVD-total colouring with } n \text{ colours}\},
\]
taken to be $\infty$ if no such colouring exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Star clique]\label{lem:star}
For every vertex $w$, the star at $w$ (namely $w$ together with its incident
edges) is a clique of $T(H)$ with $\Deg_H(w) + 1$ vertices. Hence in any total
colouring $|C(w)| = \Deg_H(w) + 1$, and $\chi''(H) \ge \Delta(H) + 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The vertex $w$ is incident to each of its edges, and any two edges at $w$ share
$w$; so the star is a clique of size $\Deg_H(w) + 1$. A proper colouring injects
a clique into the palette. Taking $w$ of maximum degree gives the bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Central graph]
The \emph{central graph} $C(G)$ has vertex set $V \sqcup E$, with: original
vertices $u,w$ adjacent iff $u \ne w$ and $uw \notin E$; an original vertex $u$
and an edge $e$ adjacent iff $u \in e$; two edges never adjacent.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Degrees in $C(G)$]\label{lem:deg}
In $C(G)$ every subdivision vertex has degree $2$, and every original vertex $v$
has degree $|V| - 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A subdivision vertex $e = uw$ is adjacent exactly to $u$ and $w$. An original
vertex $v$ is adjacent to the $\Deg_G(v)$ subdivision vertices of its edges and
to the $|V| - 1 - \Deg_G(v)$ vertices non-adjacent to it in $G$, totalling
$|V| - 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Two colouring lemmas}

\begin{lemma}[Colour-set saturation]\label{lem:sat}
If $C$ is a total colouring of $H$ with a palette of size exactly
$\Deg_H(w) + 1$, then $C(w)$ is the whole palette.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:star}, $|C(w)| = \Deg_H(w) + 1$, equal to the palette size; a
full-cardinality subset of the palette is the palette.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Adjacent equal-degree obstruction]\label{lem:obstruction}
If $u,v$ are adjacent with $\Deg_H(u) = \Deg_H(v) = \Delta$, then $H$ has no
AVD-total colouring with exactly $\Delta + 1$ colours.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For such a colouring, Lemma~\ref{lem:sat} makes $C(u)$ and $C(v)$ both equal to
the whole palette, so $C(u) = C(v)$ while $u,v$ are adjacent, violating AVD.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Upward closure]\label{lem:pad}
If $n \le m$ and $H$ has an AVD-total colouring with $n$ colours, then it has one
with $m$ colours.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Post-compose the colouring with an injection $\iota : \{1,\dots,n\}
\hookrightarrow \{1,\dots,m\}$. Injectivity preserves properness, and the new
colour sets are $\iota(C(w))$; the induced map on subsets is injective, so
$C(a) \ne C(b) \iff \iota(C(a)) \ne \iota(C(b))$. Hence the padded colouring is
AVD.
\end{proof}

\section{Main results}

Fix a $d$-regular graph $G$ with $d \ge 2$ that is not complete; thus there exist
$a \ne b$ with $ab \notin E$.

\begin{theorem}[Vertex-count bound]\label{thm:count}
If $G$ is $d$-regular and not complete, then $|V(G)| \ge d + 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For non-adjacent $a \ne b$, the set $\{a,b\} \cup N_G(a)$ has $d + 2$ distinct
elements: $a \notin N_G(a)$, $b \notin N_G(a)$ (as $ab \notin E$), $a \ne b$, and
$|N_G(a)| = d$. It is contained in $V$, so $|V| \ge d + 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No $(d+2)$-colouring]\label{thm:no}
If $G$ is $d$-regular and not complete, then $C(G)$ has no AVD-total colouring
with $d + 2$ colours.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $C$ is such a colouring. The star at the original vertex $a$ is a clique
of $T(C(G))$ of size $\Deg_{C(G)}(a) + 1 = |V|$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:deg}, and
properness injects it into the palette, giving $|V| \le d + 2$; with
Theorem~\ref{thm:count} this forces $|V| = d + 2$. Then $a,b$ are adjacent in
$C(G)$ with common degree $|V| - 1 = d + 1$, and the palette has size
$d + 2 = (d+1) + 1$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:obstruction} shows $C$ cannot be AVD, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main lower bound]\label{thm:main}
If $G$ is $d$-regular ($d \ge 2$) and not complete, then every AVD-total colouring
of $C(G)$ uses at least $d + 3$ colours; equivalently
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge d + 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If an AVD-total colouring used $n \le d + 2$ colours, Lemma~\ref{lem:pad} would
pad it to one with exactly $d + 2$ colours, contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:no}.
Hence $n \ge d + 3$, and taking the infimum gives the stated bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Five-cycle]\label{thm:c5}
The five-cycle $C_5$ is $2$-regular and not complete, and every AVD-total
colouring of $C(C_5)$ uses at least $5$ colours.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each vertex of $C_5$ has degree $2$, and vertices at distance two are
non-adjacent, so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} applies with $d = 2$, giving $d + 3 = 5$.
\end{proof}

\section{Sharpness and the limits of the conjecture}

\begin{theorem}[Size-governed bound]\label{thm:size}
For any non-complete graph $G$, $\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For non-adjacent $a \ne b$, the vertices $a,b$ are adjacent in $C(G)$ and both
have degree $|V| - 1 = \Delta(C(G))$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:deg}. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:obstruction} no AVD-total colouring uses only $|V|$ colours, so at
least $|V| + 1$ are needed.
\end{proof}

Since Theorem~\ref{thm:count} gives $|V| \ge d + 2$, we have $|V| + 1 \ge d + 3$;
the size-governed bound is always at least as strong as the degree bound, and
strictly stronger whenever $|V| > d + 2$.

\begin{corollary}[Where the conjecture can hold]\label{cor:extremal}
For a $d$-regular non-complete graph $G$, the equality $\chi''_a(C(G)) = d + 3$
can hold only if $|V(G)| = d + 2$; equivalently, the complement of $G$ is a
perfect matching, so $G = K_{d+2}$ minus a perfect matching (a cocktail-party
graph).
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If $|V| > d + 2$ then $\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V| + 1 \ge d + 4 > d + 3$. When
$|V| = d + 2$, each vertex is non-adjacent to exactly $|V| - 1 - d = 1$ other, so
the complement is $1$-regular.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Five-cycle revisited]
For $C_5$, $d = 2$ and $|V| = 5 > 4 = d + 2$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:size} gives
$\chi''_a(C(C_5)) \ge 6 > 5 = d + 3$. The naïve equality genuinely fails on
$C_5$.
\end{example}

\section{Discussion and future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Matching upper bound in the extremal case.} For $|V| = d + 2$
(cocktail-party graphs), construct an explicit AVD-total colouring of $C(G)$ with
$d + 3$ colours, proving $\chi''_a(C(G)) = d + 3$ there.
\item \textbf{Exact value for cycles.} Determine $\chi''_a(C(C_n))$ as a function
of $n$; the present bounds give $\ge n + 1$ and $\ge 5$.
\item \textbf{Characterisation of extremal graphs.} Formalise that, for a
$d$-regular graph, $|V| = d + 2$ iff the complement is $1$-regular.
\item \textbf{General upper bound.} Establish $\chi''_a(C(G)) \le |V(G)| + 2$, or
the exact value, for arbitrary regular $G$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For every non-complete $d$-regular graph with $d \ge 2$ we proved
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge d + 3$ via a counting bound, a degree identity, an adjacent
equal-degree obstruction, and a palette upward-closure lemma. The sharper bound
$\chi''_a(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$ confines the conjectured equality to the extremal
cocktail-party family $|V| = d + 2$ and shows it fails on the five-cycle. The
complementary upper bound remains the key open problem.

\end{document}

